# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: the telephone 1876, electric light about 1880, the motor
# car 1886, radio across the Atlantic 1901, the aeroplane 1903, television 1936, the first computer
# 1945, the World Wide Web 1991.
lesson history-inventions-in-order v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=3 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Inventions in order, and what each replaced"
  goal "Put the inventions of the last 150 years in order, name the ten years each came in, and say what older way each slowly replaced."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=1860 to=2000 step=20 years=[1876, 1886, 1903, 1936, 1945, 1991] labels=["Telephone", "Motor car", "Aeroplane", "Television", "First computer", "World Wide Web"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Each invention did an old job a new way. The telephone of 1876 let people talk far apart
      instead of writing; the motor car of 1886 took the place of the horse; electric light replaced
      candles and oil lamps. Television came in 1936, the first electronic computer in 1945, and the
      World Wide Web in 1991. None of them swept the old way away at once: horses, letters and lamps
      went on for many years.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.what-replaced k=0
    }
    show history.what-replaced k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.invention-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.invention-decade k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.invention-between k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.what-replaced k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.invention-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.invention-decade k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.invention-between k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level hard {
      show history.invention-order k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.letters-stayed k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.biggest-gap k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Every invention did an old job a new way, and the old way lasted for years beside it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that inventions came in an order, each doing an old job in a new way, and that
    change was slow: new and old lived side by side. The way in names what electric light replaced;
    the core orders inventions, names their decades and counts the years between them. The wrong
    idea to watch for is "when the telephone came, letters stopped", which the two-star question
    tests. If the child is stuck, ask what job the invention does and how people did it before.
    Off the page, choose one thing at home and find out what people used for that job a hundred
    years ago. England's key stage 2 history allows a theme "beyond 1066"; Japan's 社会 for grade
    six tells Japan's modern growth through its people and heritage (「我が国の歴史上の主な事象」, the
    course of study as read in its 2008 edition). Several dates are firsts that others dispute
    (who made the first light bulb, the first computer); the record gives the dates it uses and
    their sources.
    Russia's history programme reaches these inventions in grades eight and nine (recalled, not
    quoted here).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what electric light replaced, the inventions are far apart in time, the decades are of the telephone and the aeroplane, and the years counted are short."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the replacements are of printing and computing, the inventions are close together, the decades include 1901 and 1991, and the three-star question asks for the shortest wait."
  }
}
